# Website and App Design Alignment

- Implement the same approved task/calendar design and feature behavior in the website and existing native app. Preserve the explicitly approved responsive layouts: unified task column/calendar on desktop and iPad; full-width phone calendar, selected-day Week schedule, expandable task sheet and bottom navigation.
- A deadline with a due time is an exact-time marker in the24-hour schedule, separate from a work-duration block, on every viewport and runtime. Date-only deadlines remain clearly date-only; do not invent a due time or move timed deadlines into the top Due band on phones.
- Default Personal/no-class work blocks use light yellow. Preserve custom class colors; legacy built-in Personal colors resolve through the shared presentation material without rewriting saved class records.
- Resolve task/template class colors and default materials through the same shared source; template, drag preview and saved calendar copy must match. Preserve intentional user-selected class colors.
- Apply website/app parity at the authorized **"Publish to public"** release. Ordinary local website changes may lead the frozen native checkpoint; do not sync or build the app before that instruction. Follow `mobile-app-release-gate.mdc`.
- At that release, synchronize the approved web files into the existing Xcode project with native account/alarm/notification overlays and signing preserved. Compare canonical inputs, actual built bundle, installed version and target backend capabilities before claiming alignment.
- Test affected actions in desktop/phone/iPad browser layouts and actual native WKWebView, in local and signed-in modes where supported. Use the platform-testing procedure, recorded versions and PASS/FAIL/BLOCKED/NOT RUN outcomes. A screenshot, viewport emulation, GitHub push or build alone is not native feature parity.
- Verify cache updates and reload/relaunch persistence. Do not claim an installed phone update until that build is installed and checked.
- A server-capability warning requires checking the actual deployed API contract and required migrations on the existing account/origin. Repair or prepare the compatible backend within deployment authorization; do not hide the warning, fake availability, discard local data or silently redirect to a replacement server.
- Report website, local app source, built/installed app and deployed backend versions separately. Preserve user records and the public-deployment approval boundary.

- Explicit native exception (November 15, 2026): when a signed-in app server lacks the library contract, enable account/API-scoped device template CRUD and task creation/scheduling. Label it device-only and automatically import into the same account once support is detected. Preserve retry receipts, ownership and saved data; resources remain server-gated. This does not authorize a replacement backend.
